 "label": "the physical dimension of every governed numeric column",
 "note": "Reviewed declarations. build_hdf5_mapping.py used to READ THE UNIT OFF THE SPELLING of the column name, matching a 30-entry suffix table: 91 of 162 numeric columns came out 'unresolved' -- including every heat-source density, every gas density and every entropy in nats, whose units are written in their own names -- and three came out WRONG, because a suffix match is not a unit. gradient_K_per_m ends in '_m' and was published as METRES; dose_flux_open_m2_s and dose_flux_closed_m2_s end in '_s' and were published as SECONDS. The count of unresolved columns was reported by validate_hdf5_equivalence.py and gated nothing. D-2026-57. There is no 'unresolved' in this vocabulary. A column that carries no unit says why: DIMENSIONLESS is a physical ratio, COUNT is a count or index, ORDINAL is a rank-scale score with no physical dimension, and PER_ROW is a long-format table where the unit belongs to the row -- and such an entry names the column that carries it, so 'the unit is in the row' is checked rather than asserted.",
 "schema_version": 1,
 "vocabulary": {
  "COUNT": "an integer count or index; a cycle number, a step, a rank",
  "DIMENSIONLESS": "a genuine physical ratio: a fraction, a coverage, a contrast, an SNR, an attenuation",
  "ORDINAL": "a rank-scale score with no physical dimension. cost, duration and risk are {1,2,3} difficulty scores, NOT currency or time, which is exactly the misreading a suffix rule would invite",
  "PER_ROW": "the unit belongs to the row, not the column; unit_from names the column that carries it"
 }
}
